		<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-03bf516 e-con-full e-flex e-con e-child" data-id="03bf516" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
		<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-89f51ac e-con-full e-flex e-con e-child" data-id="89f51ac" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
				</div>
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-6f6cf87 elementor-widget elementor-widget-theme-post-content" data-id="6f6cf87" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="theme-post-content.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The San Antonio Spurs must dig themselves out of a massive hole if they’re going to win the <a href="https://www.thebiglead.com/tag/nba/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">NBA</a> Finals after losing Game 2 to the New York Knicks on Friday night.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Spurs entered Game 2 of the NBA Finals in a must-win situation. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It was bad enough San Antonio allowed the <a href="https://www.espn.com/nba/recap/_/gameId/401859963" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Knicks to keep rolling with a Game 1 win</a>, but the Spurs also allowed New York to wrestle away home-court advantage in the series in a game that saw San Antonio also cough up a 14-point lead.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In Game 2, the Knicks just kept on rolling, winning their 13th-straight game in a <a href="https://www.espn.com/nba/boxscore/_/gameId/401859964">105-104 win over the Spurs</a> in San Antonio.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">With the Spurs now down 0-2 against the Knicks, we’re taking a look at the history of teams that have overcome such a deficit in the NBA Finals.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Spoiler alert:</strong> San Antonio is in big trouble.</p>



<h2 id="h-history-of-0-2-comebacks-in-nba-finals" class="wp-block-heading">History of 0-2 comebacks in NBA Finals</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Five teams in NBA history have overcome an 0-2 deficit to win the NBA Finals, but NONE of those teams did so after losing the first two games at home.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That means the San Antonio Spurs must make history in order to win the NBA Finals.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The 1969 Boston Celtics (vs. Los Angeles Lakers) and 2016 Cleveland Cavaliers (vs. Golden State Warriors) overcame their 0-2 deficits and won the NBA Finals in seven games.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>MORE: <a href="https://www.thebiglead.com/ticket-prices-game-3-nba-finals-soar-higher/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Ticket prices for Game 3 of NBA Finals soar even higher</a></strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The other three teams, the 1977 Portland Trail Blazers (vs. Philadelphia 76ers), the 2006 Miami Heat (vs. Dallas Mavericks) and 2021 Milwaukee Bucks (vs. Phoenix Suns) won in six games.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Outside of the NBA Finals, five teams in league history have overcome an 0-2 deficit in the playoffs after losing two home games to start a series: the 1969 Lakers, the 1994 Houston Rockets, the 2005 Dallas Mavericks, the 2017 Boston Celtics and the 2021 Los Angeles Clippers.</p>



<h2 id="h-spurs-must-overcome-more-than-just-history" class="wp-block-heading">Spurs must overcome more than just history</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img data-od-added-loading data-od-removed-fetchpriority="high" data-od-replaced-sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" data-od-xpath="/HTML/BODY/DIV[@class=&apos;elementor elementor-68004 elementor-location-single post-181857 post type-post status-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail hentry category-nba tag-nba tag-nba-finals tag-new-york-knicks tag-san-antonio-spurs mv-content-wrapper&apos;]/*[3][self::DIV]/*[1][self::DIV]/*[2][self::DIV]/*[2][self::DIV]/*[1][self::DIV]/*[15][self::FIGURE]/*[1][self::IMG]" loading="lazy"   decoding="async" width="1024" height="683" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" src="https://www.thebiglead.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/USATSI_27947565-1024x683.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-114100" srcset="https://www.thebiglead.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/USATSI_27947565-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://www.thebiglead.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/USATSI_27947565-300x200.jpg 300w, https://www.thebiglead.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/USATSI_27947565-768x512.jpg 768w, https://www.thebiglead.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/USATSI_27947565-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://www.thebiglead.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/USATSI_27947565-2048x1365.jpg 2048w" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Jan 6, 2026; Memphis, Tennessee, USA; San Antonio Spurs forward Victor Wembanyama (1) huddles with his teammates during a timeout during the third quarter against the Memphis Grizzlies at FedExForum. Mandatory Credit: Petre Thomas-Imagn Images</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">As if the Spurs weren’t facing enough of an uphill climb, they have to pull off a historic comeback against a Knicks team playing at a historic level.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There isn’t a shred of momentum in San Antonio’s locker room after it dropped the first two games at home.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Meanwhile, New York’s locker room is filled to the brim with momentum.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Adding to all that, Madison Square Garden is a tough enough place to play at any time of the year, but it’s going to be next-level electric in the Knicks’ home arena with the team just two wins away from its first NBA title in 53 years.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"></p>
				</div>
